WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.320
Let's get stuck in early doors.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.040
Why is football so full of clichés?

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.200
Broadly speaking, there are two types
of football clichés.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.880
Firstly, there are the hypertruths -

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.840
phrases that are so obvious that
they're not even worth repeating.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:18.960
or...

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.800
These hypertruths have persisted

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.200
because they are comforting,
reliable filler.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.520
The footballing equivalent of say,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.440
talking about the weather
with your in-laws.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.440
Then there is the received wisdom -

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.800
football phrases
that have become accepted as true

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.480
despite having almost
no basis in logic.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.240
When a fan repeats such myths
as, "Some people say..."

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:41.760
or that...

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.960
it taps into their most primal fear -

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.000
that their team will suddenly
and spectacularly let them down.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.320
Paranoia and pessimism are,
after all,

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.160
central to the enjoyment of football.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.680
For all that the English language
of football has evolved

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.000
into something rather ornate
over the last 150 years,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.080
other countries have embraced
their more poetic side.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.280
What we might describe as...

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.040
that is the extreme
top corner of the goal,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.880
the Brazilians might call
'onde a dorme coruja', literally -

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.640
where the owl sleeps.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.600
While English has countless ways

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.240
to describe the fundamental
act of scoring a goal,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.080
none are quite as evocative
as the French -

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.080
'faire trembler les filets' -

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.640
make the nets quiver.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:25.840
Suddenly...

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.200
doesn’t quite sound so impressive.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.520
Football commentary is awash
with bizarre words and phrases

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.240
borrowed from other walks of life,
often from decades ago.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.320
When else in their day to day life

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.520
would the average football fan
use the words...

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.080
...that is a goal scored tidily
but with a flourish.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:44.960
Or...

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.760
...once used for 18th century pirates

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.240
but now for a defender who has
a habit of suddenly bursting upfield

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:52.240
to join the attack.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.280
The development
of the football lexicon

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.360
has largely been a one-way street

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.800
but it has given the occasional word
or phrase back

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.320
to the wider English language.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.200
…some claim originates from the
BBC's radio coverage in the 1920s,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.680
when a commentator would
refer to a grid system,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.080
printed in the Radio Times,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.800
to indicate where the ball was
on the pitch at that moment.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.120
Despite the negative connotation,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.160
clichés do have a useful function.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.360
They exist to fill gaps,
to lubricate debate

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.720
and to act as a conversational
leveller between a novice

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:24.880
...and an expert.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.440
So with the saturation
of football coverage

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.800
spare a thought
for the much maligned cliché

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:30.680
because...

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.380
where would we be without them?

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.560
Thanks for watching! :)

